Tiny Code Interpreter
====================

This backend emits and interprets packed 32-bit instructions. Each instruction
contains an 8-bit opcode and up to six 4-bit register operands. Branches and
constant-pool references use signed 20-bit byte displacements from the following
instruction. Large constants and helper descriptors are pooled after the code.

The emitter, interpreter and matching debug disassembler are adapted from QEMU
v6.2.0:
  https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu/-/tree/v6.2.0/tcg

The packed encoding was introduced by Richard Henderson in:
  650898891837a9772a5410e73c637a55d99e1e50
  "tcg/tci: Change encoding to uint32_t units"

The store emitter also includes the out-of-range offset handling from:
  2ccf40f00e3f29d85d4ff48a9a98870059002290
  "tcg/tci: Use tcg_out_ldst in tcg_out_st"

Integration with the QEMU 5 translation core
------------------------------------------

The target supplies the older per-op constraint interface and bswap semantics.
Register 13 is reserved for emitter temporaries; register 14 holds the CPU
environment and register 15 points above the spill frame. goto_tb uses indirect
chaining. goto_ptr can return through the emitted exit epilogue.

Helper calls retain the uniform uint64_t wrapper ABI in exec/helper-tci.h.
Arguments use the existing call-register order, with i64 values split into two
words on 32-bit hosts. Each call's pool entry holds the wrapper pointer followed
by its word count in bits 0..7 and argument/return size mask in bits 8 and above.
Only return registers are clobbered by a wrapper. This integration does not use
libffi.

The interpreter maintains the current bytecode return address for architectural
state restoration. A helper's normal exit stops the interpreter before another
bytecode operation executes. Guest loads and stores retain the no-flags TLB
fast path, with the existing helpers handling intercepted, MMIO, dirty, code,
unaligned and missing mappings.

Validation
----------

The ARM test target covers architectural behavior and hooks. test_tci adds
constant-pool loads, integer arithmetic, conditional branches, signed and
doubleword memory accesses, and floating-point helpers with 64-bit arguments
and results. Run both on native and wasm32 builds: wasm32 exercises split
helper arguments and paired registers that native 64-bit builds do not use.
